\details{
The first two put the design into an effective sample size, the size
of a simple random sample with the same variance,
\code{ne = p (1 - p) / se^2}, corrected for the degrees of freedom of the
design (Korn and Graubard 1998):
\verb{ne* = ne (t(n - 1) / t(df))^2}, with \code{t()} the quantile of Student's t
at the level. With \code{trunc}, the default, \verb{ne*} is never above \code{n}, as
Korn and Graubard and SAS have it; \code{survey::svyciprop()} does not
truncate, and \code{trunc = FALSE} gives its interval. An estimate of
exactly 0 or 1, or a standard error of 0, has no effective size of its
own and takes \code{n}.

\code{"beta"}: the Clopper-Pearson interval at \verb{ne*} with \verb{p ne*} events, by
beta quantiles (Korn and Graubard 1998): the "modified Clopper-Pearson"
interval. \code{"wilson"}: the Wilson score interval at \verb{ne*} with the
normal quantile: the "modified Wilson" interval (Dean and Pagano 2015).
\code{"logit"}: the Wald interval of \code{log(p / (1 - p))}, with standard error
\code{se / (p (1 - p))} and Student's t on \code{df}, taken back to a proportion;
it does not exist at 0 or 1. \code{"wald"}: \code{p} plus and minus the t
quantile times \code{se}, the interval \code{\link[=svymx]{svymx()}} gives.

Health Canada's guides to the Canadian surveys of alcohol and drug use
recommend, in this order, the modified Wilson, the modified
Clopper-Pearson and the logit interval, and advise against the Wald
interval (Health Canada 2022, section 7.3).

Errors: \code{est} outside 0 to 1; \code{se} negative; \code{n} not a whole number of
at least 2; \code{df} below 1; \code{level} outside (0, 1); \code{method} not one of
the four; \code{trunc} not \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}; arguments that are not numeric
or of different lengths (a length of 1 is repeated).
